  "text": "עצמאי\n### Step 1: Understand the Problem\nWe need to find the locus of the centers \\((a,b)\\) of all circles that intersect the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at two distinct points, such that the line segment connecting these points is perpendicular to the tangent line to the parabola at its midpoint.\n\n### Step 2: Equation of the Tangent to the Parabola\nFirst, find the equation of the tangent to the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) at a general point \\((x, y)\\). The slope of the tangent at \\((x, y)\\) is given by the derivative:\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{y}\n\\]\nSo, the equation of the tangent line at \\((x, y)\\) is:\n\\[\ny - y = \\frac{2}{y}(x - x)\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2}{y}x\n\\]\nor\n\\[\nxy = y^2\n\\]\nSince we are at a specific point on the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\), the tangent line equation becomes:\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{\\frac{4}{2}}y = \\frac{x}{2}y\n\\]\nThis means the tangent line at any point on the parabola \\(y^2 = 4x\\) always passes through the origin \\((0,0)\\).\n\n### Step 3: Midpoint of the Prolongations\nLet the two points of intersection of the circle with the parabola be \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\). The middle point of this circle is the midpoint of \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\), which is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Perpendicular Condition\nGiven that the line segment joining these two points is perpendicular, its slope \\(m_1\\) is defined by:\n\\[\nm_1 = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular line line which is given our parallelo transfom the circle tangent to the OriginS the point \\((0, 0)\\) always through the origin since our parallel transformation contains\\( a \\) Will then uniqueulo the point not suchThereforeis \\( (0 0) \\))\n\\((0 ,0)\\))\n\nosing there a ratio ofunits pointthe\\),\nthere is a ratio of this various or- }\\),\twhere the ration sineto versa by the Parabola\ner as a extension v? tou is shown 